" "q-317825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-317825" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-30"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-12008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ k\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={k}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-170647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac4f50b8f701b6dffb9c4cd99045845bedf2eb03b2e18e969709210cedffd701"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "q-8022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/state.json" "file_sha256" "acc0de8d030b386af044b586d1aafbd40539fa3a3057430467a9f381371e0306"} {"question" "e-5428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ then find $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5428.json" "file_sha256" "4526a02300da3c7a3987b250ee32e168daf6a96074b6d7ea8540d282e611f43e"} {"question" "e-5428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]) & =T(6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5428.json" "file_sha256" "4526a02300da3c7a3987b250ee32e168daf6a96074b6d7ea8540d282e611f43e"} {"question" "e-5429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5429.json" "file_sha256" "153b72b231c6cd5e4149d366e877adbefb5b41069933d9b1f3e3c42cf848032a"} {"question" "e-5429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] & =4T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5429.json" "file_sha256" "153b72b231c6cd5e4149d366e877adbefb5b41069933d9b1f3e3c42cf848032a"} {"question" "e-5430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5430.json" "file_sha256" "756e51acff0fd1d667922ad4e58e7a1804c5c110ba2752cedb63ce58e2181e7d"} {"question" "e-5430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0},$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =5{v}_{1}+12{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12(0) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5430.json" "file_sha256" "756e51acff0fd1d667922ad4e58e7a1804c5c110ba2752cedb63ce58e2181e7d"} {"question" "e-5427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Are the following transformations linear?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5427.json" "file_sha256" "8b9191b25a333eae968e74234d162bc1a5a8ce4cf57f6496d96436a473b2e5d3"} {"question" "e-5427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1.$ Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne k{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2.$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3kx \\\\ kx+2ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(3x) \\\\ k(x+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+2{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039534/example-5427.json" "file_sha256" "8b9191b25a333eae968e74234d162bc1a5a8ce4cf57f6496d96436a473b2e5d3"}] "usage" {"q-37871" [] "e-5428" [] "q-37870" [] "q-317817" [] "q-317722" [] "q-317833" [] "q-12008" [] "e-5427" [] "e-5429" [] "q-170638" [] "e-5430" [] "q-8022" []} "basis" 1367 "retractions" [[17592186088089 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$. If $T(2v_{1} + v_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -19\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(v_{1})$."] [17592186087498 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186087498 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nNoting that $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}) &= T(6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) = 6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087499 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)$."] [17592186087499 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT(kv + w) = T(kv) + T(w) = kT(v) + T(w)\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(4v_{1} + v_{2}) &= 4T(v_{1}) + T(v_{2}) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} &= 4T(v_{1}) + \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087500 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)$."] [17592186087500 :question/worked-solution "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17v_{1} + 12v_{2} &= 5v_{1} + 12v_{1} + 12v_{2} \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(v_{1} + v_{2}) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(0) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1)\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5v_{1}) &= 5T(v_{1}) \\\\\n&= 5\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186087497 :question/problem "Are the following transformations linear?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186087497 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\nT\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} = kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},; T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1$. Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}k^{2}x^{2} \\\\ k^{2}xy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= k\\begin{bmatrix}kx^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\ne kT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2$.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3kx \\\\ kx + 2ky\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}k(3x) \\\\ k(x + 2y)\\end{bmatrix}; = k\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}; = kT_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3(x_{1} + x_{2}) \\\\ (x_{1} + x_{2}) + 2(y_{1} + y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} + 3x_{2} \\\\ (x_{1} + 2y_{1}) + (x_{2} + 2y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} \\\\ x_{1} + 2y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} + 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}; = T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation."]] "immutable_ans